From 6df4473bfc87e2af337449c3d2fc72cd9661ce36 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 17:54:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] estuary: GPU time, headroom, way of drawing, frame ahead and
 step-down on the Dolby Vision page

Whether native Dolby Vision keeps up on a GPU, and what a fidelity
step-down saves there, could only be read in kodi.log. With native
output, the Dolby Vision page of the player information now shows:
- the average GPU time of a frame in the last 10 s, as the statistics
  of the log give it, and the headroom it leaves of a refresh period
  (negative: a frame takes longer than a refresh). All frames count,
  those with GUI elements too: this page is one, and while it is open
  its composition is part of the time;
- how the last frame without GUI elements was drawn, directly or
  composed (frames with GUI elements, this page among them, are always
  composed; left out of the row until there is such a frame);
- whether frames are presented a frame ahead;
- the fidelity step-down level.

Each column of the page had six rows of 50 pixels, which filled its
panel. The rows are 44 pixels now, seven to a column within the panel
(the last one ends at 104, the panel at 110), and L5 moves to the left
column, after the other levels of the metadata. With Estuary's font
(Noto Sans at 33 px), en_gb and es_es, and the widest values that are
real, every row of the page fits its 885 pixels: the widest new one is
the Spanish one of the way of drawing, 831 pixels (856 with "Yes" in
the place of Kodi's own Spanish word, which is not in its source tree);
the widest of the page, 858, is the Spanish output row of before. No string in the arguments of an $INFO or a $VAR has a comma.
Spanish texts in es_es, es_ar and es_mx. Not seen on a screen: Kodi's
GUI does not start without a GPU.
